\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Gluing conditional boundary amplitudes}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Let #{X_1,Y,X_2} be finite products of a compact metrizable
group, with their respective product Haar probabilities
#{\nu_1,\nu_\partial,\nu_2}. The boundary configuration
#{y\in Y} is retained as one shared variable. Suppose the
fine weight actually factors as
##{w(x_1,y,x_2)
 =w_1(x_1,y)w_\partial(y)w_2(x_2,y).}
Assume #{w_i\ge0} is Borel and integrable on
#{X_i\times Y}, #{w_\partial\ge0} is finite and Borel, and
#{0<Z=\int w\,d(\nu_1\otimes\nu_\partial\otimes\nu_2)<\infty}.
Let
##{A_i(y)=\int_{X_i}w_i(x_i,y)\,d\nu_i(x_i)}
where this is finite, assigning zero on the null exceptional
set. Then the unnormalized retained boundary law and its mass are
##{A_1(y)w_\partial(y)A_2(y)\,d\nu_\partial(y),\qquad
 Z=\int_Y A_1w_\partial A_2\,d\nu_\partial.}
For complex factors the same conclusion holds if each #{w_i}
is integrable on #{X_i\times Y}, #{w_\partial} is finite
Borel, and the full product is absolutely integrable;
normalization as a probability additionally requires a
nonnegative law and positive mass.}

\proof{
\p{Tonelli makes the two conditional amplitudes finite
outside a boundary null set, by the assumed integrability of
each factor. Outside that set, iterating the two independent
bulk integrals gives #{A_1w_\partial A_2}. The exceptional
set has zero product-reference measure, so assigning zero
there changes no integral. This specifies the formula
without an undefined #{0\cdot\infty}. Testing a boundary
indicator gives the asserted law, and testing one gives
#{Z}. In the complex case, slice integrability of each factor
and absolute integrability of the full product permit the
same calculation by Fubini. This is the projection case of
the conditional integration formula in
\citet{Theorem 6.4, p. 108}{kallenberg2002foundations}
and \ref{fdip-00MQ}.}

\p{For weights written as exponentials of actions, the
factorization means
#{S=S_1(x_1,y)+S_\partial(y)+S_2(x_2,y)}. Every shared
boundary interaction is assigned once, to #{S_\partial}
or to one of the two specified pieces. An interaction
depending genuinely on both #{x_1} and #{x_2} is not
covered unless it admits the stated factorization.}
}

\p{For a boundary gauge assertion, assume a compact subgroup
#{H} acts continuously on #{Y} and each #{X_i}, preserves
their Haar probabilities, and each factor satisfies
#{w_i(h\cdot x_i,h\cdot y)=w_i(x_i,y)}.
Changing the bulk integration variable gives
#{A_i(h\cdot y)=A_i(y)} wherever the defining integral is
finite; its exceptional set is invariant, so the zero
extension preserves this equality. This assumption concerns
each factor, not merely the full weight. If #{w_\partial}
is also invariant, the boundary law is invariant.}

\p{The same matching variable #{y} is integrated once.
When a boundary amplitude is bounded Borel, the Haar
projector of \ref{fdip-00L4} fixes it under the preceding
invariance hypothesis. Inserting that projector therefore
changes neither the amplitude nor the gluing integral.
Nested bulk and boundary gauge averages use
#{P_\Gamma P_H=P_\Gamma}, as in that theorem.
For unbounded amplitudes the integral formula above uses
Tonelli or Fubini; it does not treat them as elements of the
bounded-function algebra. These retained-variable statements
do not replace boundary data by separate orbit classes.}
